Release the GIL while a connection closes.

The close of the last connection to a WAL database checkpoints, and
the checkpoint fsyncs. apsw called sqlite3_close with the GIL held, so
a slow disk froze every Python thread for the checkpoint, from close()
and from the destructor alike. Lillecarl/pymux#939, Lillecarl/apsw#2.

The connection's db and mutex are taken off the object before the
call, so no other thread can reach it, and every callback a close can
reach takes the GIL itself.

--- a/src/connection.c
+++ b/src/connection.c
@@ -556,7 +556,8 @@

   for (int count = 0; count < 100; count++)
   {
-    res = sqlite3_close(db);
+    Py_BEGIN_ALLOW_THREADS res = sqlite3_close(db);
+    Py_END_ALLOW_THREADS;
     if (res == SQLITE_BUSY)
     {
       /* we can be racing with a destructor such as cursor which is
@@ -572,7 +573,8 @@
     /* we consider the connection closed but are unable to close it.  the likely
        explanation is problems with a dependent, or an extension.  the former
        won't happen unless we run out of memory */
-    sqlite3_close_v2(db);
+    Py_BEGIN_ALLOW_THREADS sqlite3_close_v2(db);
+    Py_END_ALLOW_THREADS;

     SET_EXC(res, NULL);
     if (force == 2)
